This licence is written in plain words on purpose. Each numbered section is a term of the
licence. Using the Software means you accept these terms.

1. Who is who.

   "The Licensor" is the copyright holder of the Software, named in the copyright notice
   above. "The Software" is the Oltrem kit: the code, files, documentation and packages that
   carry this licence, in any form. "You" means the person or organisation using the Software.
   An organisation includes every company that controls it, that it controls, or that is under
   common control with it.

2. What you may always do.

   You may download, install and run the Software, and keep copies of it, for Personal Use:
   use by you as an individual, for yourself, not for or on behalf of any business or other
   organisation, and not in exchange for money. You may also read the Software to understand
   how it works.

3. What a small organisation may do.

   You may use the Software for the benefit of your organisation, including in a business, if
   your organisation's gross revenue (for a non-profit, a school or a public body: its total
   income) in the previous twelve months was below one million United States dollars
   (USD 1,000,000), counting every company described in section 1 together. Revenue in another
   currency is converted at the exchange rate on the last day of that period. Using the
   Software to run anything for other businesses or people counts as your own use, and your
   revenue is what counts.

4. Above the ceiling.

   If your organisation's revenue is at or above the ceiling in section 3, your use needs a
   Commercial Licence: a separate written agreement with the Licensor, on the terms the
   Licensor publishes or agrees at the time, which may include a fee or a usage charge. If your
   revenue crosses the ceiling, you have ninety days to obtain a Commercial Licence or to stop
   using the Software.

5. No changes without permission.

   You may not change the Software: not its code, not its files, not its documentation, not in
   part and not in whole, not even privately or on your own machine, and you may not build
   anything from a changed copy, without the Licensor's prior written permission. To ask, send
   the proposed change to the Licensor at the contact address the Licensor publishes on
   oltrem.com or in the Software's README; the Licensor may review it, and may grant, refuse or
   condition permission. The Licensor's silence is a refusal. Where the law of your country
   gives you a right to study, test or decompile the Software for interoperability, or to
   correct an error that stops the Software from working as documented, this section does not
   take that right away; it does require you to tell the Licensor about any such correction and
   to apply nothing else.

6. Your permitted changes belong to the kit too.

   If the Licensor permits a change, you grant the Licensor a permanent, worldwide,
   royalty-free licence to use, change, publish and distribute that change as part of the
   Software, under this licence or any other. You keep your copyright in it.

7. What is use, not change.

   Configuring the Software through its documented configuration files, settings and client
   file; creating a client repository from the Software's template; and writing your own code
   that works with the Software through its documented interfaces, without changing any of the
